# THE MIXIN ENCODING of inheritance in lambda plus set, with no label: a
# coproduct of wrappers, read by self-application, with no helper at run
# time. A mixin is a list of functions, mutually recursive by currying, and
# mixins inherit by a plain set {m1, m2, m3}. The object is read by
# self-application, o o, as MIXINv8 does, not by Y, which cannot late-bind a
# set; o o is a Cartesian product that lets every method see every method.
# The mixin is a coproduct, which needs no generator, is short with
# self-application, and gives every method in one instantiation; its case
# is inlined as continuation passing, since an encoding with no run-time
# support decides how far mixins are first-class. Two implementations of
# one slot give two sets of answers, which is the intended meaning.
#
# - A HIERARCHY has a fixed width W, the number of its slots, each slot
#   with a fixed meaning, its position (`hierarchy/2`, multifile: a family
#   declares its own).
# - A WRAPPER is one definition under the this parameter,
#       \self. \c1. ... \cW. cm b,
#   slot m defined by b, a term over self: MIXINv8's element
#   template(Tag, Label : Body), a Scott injection whose position stands
#   for the label.
# - A MIXIN, a CLASS and an OBJECT are sets of wrappers, and inheritance is
#   the set: {Base, Mixin, ...}, a set nested in a set yielding its
#   elements, so a diamond needs nothing more and a mixin writes only the
#   slots it defines. An object is its class: nothing is instantiated and
#   no fixed point is taken, so the union of two objects is an object
#   whose self is the union (late binding over sets). A value a
#   construction site gives (a predecessor, a half) is one more wrapper in
#   the set, which the class's methods read through self.
# - READING slot m of an object o is the self-application
#       o o c1 ... cW,
#   cm the handler of the definition and every other c the empty set [],
#   which has no element to apply to a definition of another slot: the
#   first o chooses a wrapper, the second is passed to it as self (so
#   every method sees every method, as MIXINv8's projection X / L
#   supplies X as the this argument of X's own templates), and the
#   wrapper hands its body to its slot's handler. The case is written out
#   at every read, not let bound, so no helper runs; the W - 1 empty sets
#   it repeats are the position spelled out, which a calculus with no
#   label can express only by repeating the others. The handler is the
#   identity, or, where the read heads an application, \v. v a1 ... ak,
#   the application itself (continuation passing), which makes the same
#   choice at the same time. A read is never given a larger continuation:
#   that would move the choice out of the branch or the argument that
#   makes it. Inside a body, self.k is self self c1 ... cW, so self is
#   late bound, and a slot two wrappers define answers both, chosen again
#   at every read (run-time choice: each occurrence of a set chooses on
#   its own).
# - A class nested in another is built inside a body of the outer, so the
#   outer's self is in scope in its wrappers; self as a value is the
#   binder itself.
#
# The library modules are let bound at the top of a program, (\x. B) V, so
# that a program grows linearly, and an object that is not a variable is
# let bound where it is read, (\o. o o c1 ... cW) O, as the surface syntax
# holds each binder once.
#
# A LIBRARY is a MODULE: an object whose slots are the library's classes.
# Each file of the library is one mixin of the module, a wrapper of each
# class it contributes to, whose body is the contribution, a class over
# the module's self. So reading class i from the module answers the union
# of the contributions, MIXINv2's deep merge of the files, with no rule for it. A
# method that makes a value makes an object of a class read from the
# module.
#
# THE LIBRARIES, MIXINv2's (MIXINv2/packages/mixinv2-library/src/
# mixinv2_library/Builtin/ of the MIXINv2 repository), whose labeled ports were the
# retired contest_inheritance.pl and contest_inheritance_binary.pl:
# - `natural`: the classes true, false, zero and successor. BooleanData,
#   BooleanNegation, BooleanAnd and BooleanEquality contribute to true
#   and false; NatData, NatVisitor, NatPlus and NatEquality to zero and
#   successor.
# - `binary`: the classes true, false, zero, even and odd. BooleanData
#   contributes to true and false; BinNatData, BinNatIncrement,
#   BinNatVisitor, BinNatPlus and BinNatEquality to zero, even and odd,
#   and BinNatTimes, which the labeled port wrote and MIXINv2 has not.
# A Boolean has the slots church (\t. \f. t or \t. \f. f), not,
# conjunction (a function of the other operand) and equality (likewise).
# A natural has scott (\z. \s. z, or \z. \s. s p with p the predecessor's
# scott), accept (a visitor, \on_zero. \on_successor. ..., the successor
# case given the predecessor object), plus (a function of the addend,
# giving the sum), equality (a function of the other, giving a Boolean)
# and predecessor. A binary natural (zero, even 2h carrying its half h,
# odd 2h + 1 carrying its half_of_predecessor h) has increment, accept
# (\on_zero. \on_even. \on_odd. ...), plus, times (a function of the
# multiplicand), equality, half and half_of_predecessor. No library file
# implements predecessor, half or half_of_predecessor: as in MIXINv2's
# `[successor, predecessor : P]`, the site that makes the value adds a
# wrapper that implements it, and the class's methods read it through
# self.
#!  hierarchy(?Hierarchy, ?Slots) is nondet.
#
#   Hierarchy's slot names, in order; its width is their count. Multifile:
#   a family declares its own hierarchies.
#
# Each row is a file of mixin_library/hierarchies, imported by omega, so
# that mixin_library/library_module names a hierarchy by the row itself.
# FIXME: the import projects "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
